TI  - Endobronchial valves for advanced emphysema: an endobronchial follow-up.
PG  - 47-50
LID - 10.1097/LBR.0000000000000035 [doi]
AB  - Lung volume reduction surgery is a therapeutic option for selected patients with 
      advanced emphysema. However, it is an invasive procedure benefitting only a 
      selected group of patients with heterogeneous upper lobe predominant disease and 
      limited exercise capacity. The most widely studied alternatives are endobronchial 
      valves. Hundreds of patients worldwide have undergone endobronchial valve 
      placement. Although short-term outcomes have been described, little is known 
      about the long-term side effects following this treatment, and endoscopic 
      follow-up is virtually nonexistent. The images, biopsies, and microbiologic 
      evidence accrued from this patient are witnesses to the natural evolution of 
      endobronchial valves in the airways and should offer a word of caution with 
      regard to valve placement in patients with life expectancies exceeding those 
      typical of severe chronic obstructive lung disease.
