TI  - Recurrent lung cavities and endobronchial nodules in an adolescent male.
PG  - 66-7
AB  - Multiple lung cavitations and endobronchial nodules are rare presentations of 
      newly diagnosed and recurrent Hodgkin's disease. The clinical and radiological 
      features can be confused with pulmonary tuberculosis, which can be difficult to 
      exclude in endemic areas. However, the presence of endobronchial nodules point, 
      towards Hodgkin's disease. Differential diagnosis is aided by the fact that these 
      lesions usually respond promptly to specific therapy. We present a case of an 
      adolescent male who had constitutional and pulmonary symptoms associated with 
      pulmonary cavities and endobronchial nodules subsequently confirmed to be 
      Hodgkin's disease.
