TI  - Phenotypic characterization of chronic inflammation in a rare case of 
      endobronchial carcinoma.
PG  - 1411-6
LID - 10.1007/s00262-010-0861-x [doi]
AB  - This report presents a phenotypical characterization of the immune cell 
      infiltrate in a rare case of endobronchial carcinoma. A patient initially treated 
      for an adenocarcinoma of the esophagus developed an endobronchial carcinoma 
      surrounded by gastric metaplasia distal to a suspected gastrobronchial fistula, 
      11 years after esophagectomy. Our hypothesis is that the sustained exposure of 
      the bronchial mucosa to a mixed acid and pancreatobiliary refluxate led to 
      chronic inflammation and promoted malignant transformation. We performed an 
      immunohistochemical study of the tumor microenvironment evaluating the density of 
      CD3(+), CD8(+) T lymphocytes, CD20(+) B lymphocytes, CD68(+) macrophages and 
      FoxP3(+) regulatory T cells. Quantification of immune cell density was completed 
      using a novel software-based analysis method. Our results suggest that, within 
      all the tissues analyzed, FoxP3(+) regulatory T cells were present at their 
      highest density in the malignant and metaplastic tissues. The endobronchial 
      metaplasia biopsied several years prior to the detection of the endobronchial 
      adenocarcinoma was already densely infiltrated by B cells and macrophages, when 
      compared to the immune cell infiltrate of the endobronchial carcinoma. 
      Altogether, these observations support the current understanding of 
      carcinogenesis promoted by chronic inflammation.
