DP  - 2003 Dec
TI  - Endobronchial lesions involved in Mycobacterium avium infection.
PG  - 1261-4
AB  - Mycobacterium avium (M. avium) has been described traditionally as an 
      opportunistic organism that causes disseminated disease in the human 
      immunodeficiency virus (HIV)-positive population and that acts as a pulmonary 
      pathogen in patients with underlying lung disease such as chronic obstructive 
      pulmonary disease (COPD) or previously diagnosed tuberculosis. Pulmonary 
      involvement of M. avium may range from asymptomatic colonization of the airway to 
      invasive parenchymal or cavitary disease. However, endobronchial lesions involved 
      in M. avium infection are rare in either immunocompetent or immunosuppressed 
      hosts. We report here endobronchial mycobacterial infection in a HIV-negative 
      patient.
