VI  - 42
IP  - 2
DP  - 2004 Feb
TI  - [A case of endobronchial mucormycosis with peculiar bronchoscopic findings].
PG  - 181-4
AB  - Small cell lung cancer was diagnosed in a 54-year-old man in 1997. He had 
      received 5 cycles of systemic chemotherapy and thoracic irradiation since 1997, 
      and a favorable response had been achieved. In August 2001, pro-GRP was again 
      elevated, and he was readmitted. Bronchoscopic findings revealed a white 
      dendritic endobronchial mass on a cheese-like plug obstructing the left upper 
      bronchus division. Numerous mucor hyphe and cancer cells were detected by 
      transbronchial biopsy, and a fungal culture disclosed mucor. Although no 
      antifungal drug was administered, the bronchial lesion disappeared after removal 
      with biopsy forceps and 2 cycles of systemic anticancer chemotherapy. 
      Endobronchial involvement of mucormycosis is rare and this case is unique because 
      of the peculiar bronchoscopic finding with the endobronchial lesion.
