DP  - 2004 Jul
TI  - Unstable endobronchial intubation in a cat undergoing tracheal laceration repair.
PG  - 227-30
AB  - A peri-carinal tracheal laceration was produced in a 11-year-old cat during 
      tracheal intubation. Before reconstructive surgery began, the leak was bypassed 
      with an endobronchial tube positioned using endoscopy and direct vision. However, 
      single-lung ventilation could not be sustained because the tube became dislodged 
      and could not be repositioned. Consequently, surgery was completed with periods 
      of intermittent apnoea interspersed with manually controlled hyperventilation. 
      Cardiovascular variables were stable during anaesthesia and no signs of hypoxia 
      were detected. The difficulties in maintaining endobronchial tube position 
      resulted from the animal's small size relative to the dimensions of the 
      endotracheal tube.
