LR  - 20220317
IS  - 1556-4029 (Electronic)
IS  - 0022-1198 (Linking)
VI  - 59
IP  - 4
DP  - 2014 Jul
TI  - Endobronchial/tracheal metastasis and sudden death.
PG  - 1139-41
LID - 10.1111/1556-4029.12431 [doi]
AB  - Endobronchial and endotracheal metastases are rare lesions that may cause acute 
      occlusion of major airways. A case is reported of a 62-year-old man with history 
      of renal cell carcinoma and hemoptysis who died after having suddenly become 
      short of breath with cyanosis. At autopsy, recurrent renal cell carcinoma was 
      identified with metastases to the lungs, pulmonary hilar lymph nodes, and brain. 
      In addition, a solitary papillomatous metastasis arising from the mucosa of the 
      carina was obstructing both the main bronchi and distal trachea. Death was due to 
      acute airway obstruction by a metastatic deposit at the bifurcation of the 
      trachea in a case of disseminated renal cell carcinoma. This case demonstrates an 
      extremely rare cause of sudden death that may complicate disseminated malignancy.
CI  - © 2014 American Academy of Forensic Sciences.
