TI  - Chondroma: an uncommon case of bronchial tumor.
PG  - 270-3
LID - 10.1097/LBR.0b013e3181b64985 [doi]
AB  - We report the case of a patient with an endobronchial chondroma, a rare benign 
      endobronchial tumor. A 37-year-old woman was evaluated for suspicion of asthma. 
      Computed tomography of the chest and flexible bronchoscopy showed an endoluminal 
      tumor occluding the culmen (B3 left). The tumor was extirpated almost completely 
      by bronchoscopy. Histologically, the biopsy specimens were composed of 
      chondromatous tissue located under the normal bronchial mucosa, according to the 
      diagnosis of chondroma. The symptoms disappeared immediately after the procedure 
      and did not reappear after 1 year. Chondromas are usually located on the 
      skeleton. Endobronchial presentation is rare. We know of only 41 cases reported 
      in the literature. Hamartoma or chondrosarcoma constitutes the main histologic 
      differential diagnosis. The treatment consists of complete resection to avoid any 
      risk of recurrence or sarcomatous transformation. There is no consensus regarding 
      the method of treatment, but endoscopic resection seems to be the first choice.
