TI  - [Efficiency of endobronchial laser therapy in patients with chronic bronchitis].
PG  - 25-8
AB  - Clinical effectiveness of endobronchial laser therapy (EBLT) was studied in 
      18-56-year-old patients with chronic bronchitis (CB) running for 2-18 years. A 
      parallel luminescent and histochemical tests measured histamine, serotonin and 
      catecholamines in bronchial mucosa. It was found that alveolar macrophages, 
      lymphocytes and mucus of CB patients contain significantly higher amounts of 
      histamine and serotonin though low amounts of catecholamines than those of 
      healthy subjects. Levels of monoamines in alveolar macrophages, lymphocytes, 
      neutrophils, mast and APUD cells, mucus in of CB patients correlated with the 
      disease phase, duration, features of endobronchitis, bronchial obstruction and 
      severity of respiratory insufficiency. EBLT produced a positive effect on CB 
      course and bronchial mucosa bioamines. It was superior to conventional therapy in 
      lowering of histamine and serotonin in alveolar macrophages, lymphocytes and 
      mucus as well as in raising catecholamines.
