VI  - 35
IP  - 6
DP  - 2014 Dec
TI  - Convex probe endobronchial ultrasound.
PG  - 636-44
LID - 10.1055/s-0034-1395498 [doi]
AB  - Convex probe endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) is a minimally invasive diagnostic 
      technique that allows real-time sampling of mediastinal and hilar lymph nodes and 
      central pulmonary lesions. Its utility in diagnosing both malignant and 
      nonmalignant diseases has led to an increased uptake and use by pulmonologists 
      over the past decade. Because of the robust evidence supporting its safety and 
      diagnostic yield, EBUS is now the first guideline recommended test for staging in 
      non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). It has also a role in providing tissue for 
      molecular analysis, thereby guiding in the selection of agents in the new era of 
      personalized chemotherapies in the treatment of NSCLC. The following review 
      highlights the evidence for EBUS in diagnosing mediastinal pathology and 
      addresses technique, training, and competency and future directions for this 
      technology.
