DP  - 2009 Oct
TI  - [Evaluation of the relation between the depth of left double-lumen endobronchial 
      tube and the patient's height--comparison between patients over 20 years of age 
      and those under 19 years].
PG  - 1244-7
AB  - BACKGROUND: In adult patients, there is a strong correlation between the 
      appropriate depth of insertion of a left double-lumen endobronchial tube (DLT) 
      and height. However, for patients with the same height, we noted the tendency of 
      the depth of insertion being about 1 cm shorter for patients under 19 years of 
      age compared to that for those over 20 years. METHODS: We compared the depth of 
      insertion between patients over 20 years and those under 19 years. RESULTS: In 
      both groups, the depth of DLT insertion correlated strongly with patient's 
      height. For patients of the same height, the depth of insertion for those under 
      19 years was 0.7-1.2 cm shorter than that for patients over 20 years. For 
      patients with a height between 160-179 cm, the mean depth of insertion for 
      patients under 19 years was significantly shorter than that for those over 20 
      years. CONCLUSIONS: During intubation of the bronchi with DLT in patients under 
      19 years, the optimal depth of DLT insertion is less than that for patients over 
      20 years.
