DP  - 2000 Feb
TI  - Granulation stenosis caused by a Dumon stent placed for endobronchial tuberculous 
      stenosis.
PG  - 41-3
AB  - Two patients with cicatric tracheobronchial stenosis caused by tuberculosis who 
      suffered granulation stenosis after placement of a Dumon stent are reported. 
      Dumon stents, which were long enough to cover the stenotic sites, were placed in 
      the trachea and left main bronchus of each patient. Granulation tissue grew at 
      both edges of the stent 3 or 4 months after stent placement, which caused 
      restenosis and necessitated removal of the stents. The authors conclude that a 
      Dumon stent for treatment of tracheobronchial stenosis caused by tuberculosis can 
      cause granulation stenosis at the edges of the stent.
