TI  - Postoperative strategies to treat permanent air leaks.
PG  - 391-7
LID - 10.1016/j.thorsurg.2010.03.004 [doi]
AB  - Air leakage after pulmonary resections is considered the most prevalent 
      postoperative problem, and it is often the only morbidity identified. Ideally, 
      treatment begins with prevention; the onset of this complication should be 
      anticipated and recognized during surgery, and intraoperative strategies should 
      be attempted to avoid it and reduce the impact on the clinical course. Once an 
      air leak develops, in most of the cases it seals spontaneously within 2 or 3 days 
      of operation. When it persists, it might elicit the onset of other complications 
      and increase costs and length of hospitalization. The postoperative approaches to 
      a prolonged air leak include management of the pleural drainage and residual 
      space, pleurodesis, pneumoperitoneum, endobronchial one-way valve placement, and 
      potential reoperation.
