TI  - Educating the next generation of pulmonary fellows in transbronchial needle 
      aspiration. Leading the blind to see.
PG  - 828-32
LID - 10.1513/AnnalsATS.201403-112OI [doi]
AB  - Transbronchial needle aspiration (TBNA) remains an invaluable diagnostic tool in 
      the evaluation of mediastinal and hilar abnormalities, specifically in the 
      evaluation of patients with lung cancer. Training in TBNA has remained integral 
      in pulmonary fellowship programs, but unfortunately the training methods, 
      volumes, and outcomes have been variable. This has subsequently led to wide 
      variations in practice patterns, diagnostic yield, and operator confidence. The 
      introduction of endobronchial ultrasound-guided TBNA appears to have stimulated a 
      resurgence in training and performance of TBNA. However, with this new 
      technology, many questions have surfaced regarding training methods, volumes, and 
      who should receive training. Within this context, we describe the history, 
      current state, and future directions of the education of TBNA during pulmonary 
      fellowship training.
