TI  - Diagnosis of cystic fibrosis.
PG  - 100-6
LID - 10.1007/s12016-008-8078-x [doi]
AB  - Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the 
      CF transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) gene that results in abnormal 
      viscous mucoid secretions in multiple organs and whose main clinical features are 
      pancreatic insufficiency and chronic endobronchial infection. Although it was 
      initially defined and diagnosed based on clinical features and sweat chloride 
      measurement, an in vivo method of assessing CFTR function, the discovery of the 
      CFTR gene in 1989 revealed a broad spectrum of CF phenotypes associated with 
      specific CFTR gene mutations. In this article, we will review the indications for 
      sweat testing, alternative techniques to diagnose CF, and the approach to 
      patients with an ambiguous or indeterminate diagnosis of CF.
