LID - 10.1007/s11604-009-0340-5 [doi]
AB  - A 77-year-old woman who had been in the hospital suffering from a subarachnoid 
      hemorrhage suddenly died after undergoing a cerebral aneurysmal operation. 
      Postmortem whole-body computed tomography (CT) demonstrated multiple lung tumors 
      with abnormal masses in the bronchus with no evidence of complications in the 
      cranium. The patient was estimated to have died from asphyxia caused by 
      metastatic lung and endobronchial tumors. Although a traditional autopsy was not 
      performed, postmortem CT provided strong evidence for detecting the cause of 
      death. In this case, postmortem CT played an important role in hospital risk 
      management.
