TI  - Clinical presentation and evaluation of neuroendocrine tumors of the lung.
PG  - 267-76
LID - S1547-4127(14)00036-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.thorsurg.2014.04.002 [doi]
AB  - Neuroendocrine tumors of the lung encompass a wide spectrum. A carcinoid tumor is 
      either a central smooth endobronchial tumor or a round, well-circumscribed, 
      peripheral parenchymal lesion. Distinguishing typical carcinoid tumors from 
      atypical carcinoid tumors is unreliable from a limited biopsy but can be based on 
      age, presentation, and node enlargement. Large cell neuroendocrine cancer 
      presents similarly to most non-small cell lung cancers. Small cell lung cancer 
      has a characteristic presentation, with a rapid progression of symptoms, and a 
      bulky central and/or mediastinal tumor. A diagnosis is achieved by limited biopsy 
      and is usually reliable.
