TI  - Primary pulmonary mucinous (colloid) adenocarcinoma with postoperative bone 
      metastasis.
PG  - 677-81
LID - 10.5761/atcs.cr.13-00260 [doi]
AB  - We describe rare primary pulmonary mucinous (colloid) adenocarcinoma in an 
      80-year-old man. Chest computed tomography revealed a lobulated, well-defined 
      nodule with a diameter of 3.2 cm in the right middle lobe. Transbronchial biopsy 
      via endobronchial ultrasound with a guide sheath did not uncover malignancy. 
      Right middle lobectomy proceeded because the tumor was located close to the 
      pulmonary hilum. Macroscopically, the cut surface of the nodule comprised a well 
      demarcated area of somewhat transparent granular aggregates and a yellow-white 
      gelatinous substance. Computed tomography findings of a solitary metastatic 
      lesion in the left fifth costal head 28 months thereafter were consistent with 
      those of a mucin-rich tumor, which was effectively treated by radiotherapy.
