DP  - 2011 Jul
TI  - Multimodality Staging of Lung Cancer.
PG  - 251-63
LID - 10.1016/j.cpet.2011.05.001 [doi]
AB  - Computed tomography (CT) is the primary noninvasive imaging modality for staging 
      of non-small cell lung cancer. Magnetic resonance imaging compliments CT in tumor 
      staging in specific situations. [(18)F]Fluorodeoxyglucose positron emission 
      tomography/CT is useful in detecting additional regional nodal and distant 
      metastatic sites of disease, thereby reducing the number of unnecessary 
      surgeries. However, noninvasive imaging modalities lack sufficient accuracy for 
      nodal staging in all cases and, hence, preoperative lymph node sampling with 
      invasive techniques such as mediastinoscopy, thoracoscopy, and fine-needle 
      aspiration cytology guided by endobronchial ultrasound or endoscopic ultrasound 
      is frequently required in selected patients.
