TI  - Photodynamic therapy: another option in cancer treatment.
PG  - 95-9
AB  - Photodynamic therapy (PDT)--also called phototherapy, photoradiation therapy, or 
      photochemotherapy--is a relatively new treatment option being used to treat 
      certain types of cancer, including endobronchial and esophageal cancers. PDT is a 
      two-step process that involves administration of a photosensitizing agent 
      followed by exposure to non thermal laser light. Intensive patient education and 
      support are required during PDT. In the immediate post-procedure period, patients 
      require intensive monitoring to ensure that a patient airway is maintained and 
      pain is alleviated. Photosensitivity persist for four to six weeks post-PDT; to 
      avoid photosensitivity reactions, patients must adhere to specific 
      recommendations to avoid exposure to light. PDT is noted for extending the life 
      expectancy and improving the quality of life of patients.
