DP  - 2009 Oct
TI  - Anesthesia for airway surgeries under the perioperative venovenous extracorporeal 
      membrane oxygenation: Three case reports.
PG  - 522-527
LID - 10.4097/kjae.2009.57.4.522 [doi]
AB  - The airway management for patients with critical airway problems continues to be 
      a challenge to the anesthesiologist. In general cases, conventional ventilation 
      techniques have been used successfully. These include fiberoptic bronchoscope 
      guided intubation, supraglottic airway, endotracheal or endobronchial intubation 
      at operative field, high frequency jet ventilation, etc. However, patients with 
      near-fatal airway obstruction or severely depressed pulmonary function that is 
      refractory to conventional ventilation methods also present. In these cases, 
      cardiopulmonary bypass or extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) can be used. 
      Although these situations are uncommon indications for ECMO, ECMO can be a 
      potential option for these life threatening conditions. Especially, venovenous 
      (VV) ECMO can be used for pure pulmonary support. We describe three cases of 
      airway surgery requiring ECMO. VV ECMO was established in all cases. ECMO 
      provided adequate temporary pulmonary support and all patients weaned from ECMO 
      successfully without any complication.
