VI  - 36
IP  - 4
DP  - 2012 Dec
TI  - The role of videomediastinoscopy in staging of non-small cell lung cancer.
PG  - 1441-4
AB  - Lung cancer is the most frequent malignant disease and the leading cause of death 
      from malignant diseases in the world and its incidence is increasing. At the time 
      when diagnosis is established most patients have advanced disease and are not 
      candidates for radical surgical treatment. Patients without distant metastases 
      are subjected to various diagnostic methods to detect metastases in mediastinal 
      lymph nodes that make up the path of lymph drainage from the lungs. The most 
      reliable invasive diagnostic procedures for detecting metastases in mediastinal 
      lymph nodes are videomediastinoscopy and endobronchial ultrasound with 
      transtracheal puncture. In the absence of mediastinal lymph node metastases 
      surgery is the treatment of choice. If mediastinal lymph nodes are positive for 
      metastases multimodal treatment is implemented. At the Department of Thoracic 
      Surgery, Zadar General Hospital, videomediastinoscopy for the staging of primary 
      non-small cell lung cancer has been performed routinely since September 2009.
