TI  - Inhalation of TGF-beta1 antibody: a new method to inhibit the airway stenosis 
      induced by the endobronchial tuberculosis.
PG  - 1065-6
LID - 10.1016/j.mehy.2009.04.037 [doi]
AB  - Tuberculous tracheobronchial stenosis is a serious clinical problem because it 
      can cause obstructive pneumonia, dyspnea on exertion even pulmonary atelectasis 
      of the whole lung. More than 90% of the patients with EBTB have some degree of 
      bronchial stenosis. The interventional therapy through bronchoscopy has been used 
      as standard treatment to deal with the scar stenosis of EBTB routinely, which 
      have showed significant effects to keep airway open. However the scar rapid 
      growing and restenosis can often be seen in many patients. TGF-beta1 has been 
      demonstrated to play a very important role in scar formation. Some agents against 
      TGF-beta1 have been proved to inhibit the scar growing effectively. The level of 
      TGF-beta elevated in the BALF of EBTB patients suggests TGF-beta be related to 
      the pathogenesis of stenosis induced by EBTB. We hypothesize that inhalation of 
      TGF-beta1 antibody can neutralize the local TGF-beta and reduce the level of this 
      kind of cytokine so as to prevent the scar formation partially, and find a new 
      pathway to deal with this tough clinical problem.
